## 1 · Jobs

| Job | Kind | Calls | Trigger | Cadence property | On/off property | Coordination | Missed run | Per pass |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `OutboxRelayJob` | polling pass | `RelayOutboxEvents.relayPending(RelayOutboxEventsCommand)` | `fixedDelay` | `app.outbox.poll-interval: PT1S` | `app.outbox.enabled` | row claim (§ 3) | next pass is the catch-up | `app.outbox.batch-size` rows; a failing row is marked and the pass continues |
| `OutboxPruneJob` | calendar-anchored | `PruneOutboxEvents.prunePublished(PruneOutboxEventsCommand)` | `cron = "0 30 3 * * *"`, `zone = "America/Sao_Paulo"` | `app.outbox.prune.cron` | `app.outbox.prune.enabled` | lock (§ 3) | skipped — the next night deletes the backlog too | batches of `app.outbox.prune.batch-size` until a batch deletes fewer |

## 2 · Technology

| Decision | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Scheduler | `@Scheduled` | Two polling/cron jobs, no runtime-created schedules, no catch-up need, no operator UI — every must-row of `references/tool-decision-matrix.md` § 2 is met by the tool with zero infrastructure |
| Coordination tool | ShedLock (prune only) | Replicas = 2 (§ 3). The relay needs no lock — its claim partitions the work. The prune is idempotent, but two instances deleting the same batches at 03:30 double the load on the hottest table for nothing |
| Inherited? | no — first job of this project | `pom.xml` carries no scheduler; no earlier `35-jobs.md` |

Rejected, one line each: **Quartz** — eleven tables for two static jobs, and no catch-up need to
justify them. **db-scheduler** — would coordinate both jobs, but adds a scheduler where
`@Scheduled` already runs, and the relay does not need its lock. **Spring Batch** — one batch per
pass, nothing to restart from. **JobRunr** — no operator-UI requirement; LGPL not asked.

## 3 · Coordination

| Fact | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Replicas in production | 2 | asked in this run — no earlier `35-jobs.md`, no `Claim strategy` in any `20-persistence.md` |

| Job | Mechanism | Bounds |
|---|---|---|
| `OutboxRelayJob` | **Row claim**, locked or leased — required from persistence in § 6. Every instance runs the pass; each claims different rows | — |
| `OutboxPruneJob` | ShedLock `outboxPrune` | `lockAtMostFor = PT30M` (worst run observed in staging: 4 min), `lockAtLeastFor = PT1M` (clock skew between pods), database clock (`usingDbTime()`) |

## 4 · Execution guarantees

| Guarantee | Value |
|---|---|
| Body idempotent | Relay: a row sent and not marked is re-sent; the consumer's dedupe absorbs it (`25-messaging.md` § 3). Prune: deleting already-deleted rows deletes nothing |
| Per-pass bound | Relay: batch size from persistence. Prune: 1 000 rows per statement, loop until a statement deletes fewer |
| One failing item | Relay: marked failed, counted, the pass continues. Prune: a failing statement ends the pass; the next night retries |
| Missed run | Neither needs catch-up: the relay's next pass reads every pending row, the prune's next run reads every expired row |

## 5 · Observability

| Metric | Job | Alarm it exists for |
|---|---|---|
| `jobs.execution` (timer, tags `job`, `outcome`) | both | failure rate above zero for 15 min |
| `jobs.last_success.seconds` (gauge, tag `job`) | both | relay: older than 5 min · prune: older than 26 h |
| `outbox.events.dead_lettered`, `outbox.pending.age.seconds` | relay | names and alarms are `25-messaging.md`'s; `OutboxRelayJob` records them from each pass's outcome |

## 6 · Schema requirements

Read by `persistence-architect` in its first pass, which runs after this skill. Requirements,
never DDL.

| Requirement | Why | Addressee |
|---|---|---|
| Outbox claim that **locks or leases** rows | § 3: 2 replicas run the relay pass; an unlocked read publishes every batch twice | `persistence-architect` step 4b — records it as `Claim strategy` |
| `OutboxRetentionGateway.deletePublishedBefore(cutoff, limit)` — bounded delete of published rows older than a cutoff; never a pending or dead-lettered row | `OutboxPruneJob` → `PruneOutboxEvents` | `persistence-architect` step 4b — the statement and the retention window are its own |
| `shedlock` table — vendor DDL for the JDBC provider | § 2 | `persistence-architect` — as a migration; the library's own initialization stays off |

## 7 · Declared dependencies

| Dependency | Version | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `net.javacrumbs.shedlock:shedlock-spring` | (resolve at runtime — not managed by the Boot parent) | `@SchedulerLock` on the prune |
| `net.javacrumbs.shedlock:shedlock-provider-jdbc-template` | (same version as above) | Lock provider over the application's datasource |

## 8 · Configuration

| Property | Value | Owner of the value |
|---|---|---|
| `app.outbox.enabled` | `true`; `false` in `application-test.yml` | this partial |
| `app.outbox.poll-interval` | `PT1S` | this partial |
| `app.outbox.batch-size`, `app.outbox.max-attempts` | — | `20-persistence.md` |
| `app.outbox.prune.enabled` | `true`; `false` in `application-test.yml` | this partial |
| `app.outbox.prune.cron` | `0 30 3 * * *` | this partial |
| `app.outbox.prune.batch-size` | `1000` | this partial |
| `app.outbox.prune-after` | — | `20-persistence.md` § 1 (retention) — ships in this change, because its reader does |
| `spring.task.scheduling.pool.size` | not set — the build targets JDK 21, so virtual threads are on and the key is read by nothing (`.claude/rules/virtual-threads.md`). Below 21: `2`, since a pool of one would make the prune wait for the relay | this partial |

Full shape in `templates/application-jobs.yml.example`.

